@@ Comment conventions:
@@
@@ I (mostly) put functional comments before a bit of code.
@@ I often include what will happen with the stack in the form:
@@  ( <stack before> -- <stack after> ).
@@ Sometimes there is more on the stack, that can be ignored
@@  while working with the following bit of code, but is needed
@@  later. That is indicated with "..." .

@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@
@@ These first few functions where usefull while developing this.
@@ They could be removed.

@@ DB48X doesn't (yet) provide the DIAG→ command.
@@ This is a purpose targeted alternative implementation.

@@ Transform a vector to a diagonal matrix.
@@ ( vector -- matrix )
@@ 
«
	→ v
	«
		@@ Initialise the output matrix.
		v Size Head IdentityMatrix

		@@ Copy each vector element to the corresponding diagonal position.
		1  v Size Head
		For i
		    {}  i +  i +
		    v  i  Get
		    Put
		Next
	»
»
DiagonalFrom Store


@@ Generate a size n correlation matrix with random values.
@@ ( n -- matrix)
«
	@@ A generator of random correlation coefficient values.
	«
		-1.0 1.0 Random
	»

	→ n g
	«
		{} n + n + 0 ConstantArray
		
		@@ Run only over the part below the diagonal.
		2  n
		For row
			1  row 1 -
			For column
				{} row +  column +
				g Put
			Next
		Next

		@@ Make it symmetric.
		Duplicate  Transpose +
		@@ Fill in the diagonal.
		n IdentityMatrix +
	»
»
GenerateRandomCorrelationMatrix Store


@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@
@@ The real stuff begins here.
@@
@@ For an overview see (or other simular documentation):
@@ https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Propagation_of_uncertainty

@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@
@@ A few helper commands.

@@ Break up a range with units to its a and b value, with units.
@@ (<a b range>_rangeUnit -- a_aUnit b_bUnit)
«
        @@ Preserve the rangeUnit. Later attach it to a and b.
        @@ ( -- 1_rangeUnit <a b range>_rangeUnit )
        1 Over →Unit Swap

        @@ Remove the rangeUnit. Break up the range.
        @@ ( -- 1_rangeUnit a b )
        Object→ Drop Object→

        @@ Attach unit to b.
        @@ ( -- 1_rangeUnit a b_bUnit )
        Pick3 →Unit
        
        @@ Attach unit to a.
        @@ ( -- a_aUnit b_bUnit )
        UnRot Swap →Unit Swap
»
RangeWithUnitsFrom Store


@@ Zip 2 lists.
@@ ( l1 l2 -- l3 )
@@
@@ l3 will have the elements of l1 and l2 alternately.
@@ Note: unlike Python zip the resulting list is flat.
«
	→ l1 l2
	«
		@@ Initialize result.
		{}

		1  l1 size
		For i
			l1 i get +
			l2 i get +
		Next
	»
»
zipper Store

@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@

@@ Calculate the "numerical derivative".
@@ ( function { x v } -- df/dx_approximation )
@@
@@ The { x v } parameter is a list of a single function parameter with its value.
@@ 
@@ See: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Numerical_differentiation: five point midpoint approximation.
@@ The differentation approximation parameter h is not an absolute small value but h = x * d.
@@ Where d is a small value.
@@
@@ Note that we do not evaluate for x = v. v is not used at all.
@@ The calling command gets an expression back, that still needs to be evaluated.
@@ In that sense this derivative is not a completely numerical approximation.
@@ It is an algebraic approximation.
@@
@@ Example:
@@
@@ 'tgamma(x²)'
@@ { x 1 }
@@ NumericalDerivative
@@
@@ Expect: '((Gamma((X·1.00000 0001)²)-Gamma((X·0.99999 9999)²))·8+(Gamma((X·0.99999 9998)²)-Gamma((X·1.00000 0002)²)))÷(12·(X·0.00000 0001))'
«
	@@ Extra local variable(s).
	@@ ( -- d emptyProgram)
	1.0e-9
	« »

	→ f xv d generateOnePoint
	«
		@@ Generate one point.
		@@ ( w -- f((x * (1 + w * d))) )
		@@
		@@ In f(x) replace x with (x * (1 + w * d)). 
		«
			→ w
			«
				@@ ( -- f(x) )
				f
				@@ ( f(x) -- f(x) { x v } valuePosition )
				xv 2
				@@ ( ... -- x )
				xv 1 Get
				@@ ( x -- 'x * (1 + w * d)' )
				1 w d * + *
				@@ ( { x v } valuePostion 'x * (1 + w * d)' ) -- { x 'x * (1 + w * d)' } )
				Put
				@@ ( f(x) -- f((x * (1 + w * d))) )
				Where
			»
		»
		'generateOnePoint' Store

		@@ -f(x+2h) + 8 * f(x+h) -8 * f(x-h) + f(x-2h) =
		@@ (f(x+h) - f(x-h)) * 8 + (f(x-2h) - f(x+2h))
		+1 generateOnePoint
		-1 generateOnePoint
		-
		8 *
		-2 generateOnePoint
		+2 generateOnePoint
		-
		+
	
		@@ Divide by 12h. Recall that h = x * d.
		@@ Note that we are dividing by (a multiple of) x.
		@@ That could lead to dividing by 0, when x = 0.
		@@ But since we do not evaluate for p = x = v,
		@@ no harm will be done here.
		@@ It is up to the calling command to deal with x = 0.
		12
		@@ h = p * d
		xv 1 Get  d * *
		/
	»
»
NumericalDerivative Store


@@ Calculate the partial derivative of a function: df/dx[i](v[i]).
@@ ( function listOfParametersAndValues i -- partialDerivative )
«
	@@ Extra local variable(s).
	@@ ( i -- i*2 i*2-1 )
	2 *
	dup 1 -

	→ f pv parameterValueIndex parameterIndex 
	«
		@@ Prepare for later.
		@@ ( -- { x[i] v[i] } )
		pv  parameterIndex  Get
		pv  parameterValueIndex  Get
		2 ToList

		@@ Keep all x[*] but x[i] constant.
		@@ Evaluate f for all x[*] except x[i].
		@@ ( ... -- fPartialEvaluated )
		f
		@@ Take x[i] out of pv, by changing it.
		pv  parameterIndex  "~dummy~"  Put
		@@ Substitute v[*] for x[*] except for x[i] and evaluate as far as possible.	
		Where  Evaluate

		@@ Differentiate the partial evaluated function with respect to x[i]. 
		IfErr
			@@ ( fPartialEvaluated -- fPartialEvaluated x[i] )
			pv  parameterIndex  Get
			@@ ( fPartialEvaluated x[i] -- d/dx[i]_fPartialEvaluated )
			Derivative
		Then
			@@ Oops! Unknown derivative.
			@@ ( fPartialEvaluated x[i] -- fPartialEvaluated { x[i] v[i] } ) 
			Drop
			Over
			@@ ( fPartialEvaluated { x[i] v[i] } -- df/dx[i]_approximation )
			NumericalDerivative
		End

		@@ Evaluate at x[i] = v[i].
		@@ ( { x[i] v[i] }  df/dx[i] -- df/dx[i](x[i] = v[i]) )
		Swap			
		@@ Evaluate the "partial derivative" for x[i] = v[i].
		Where  Evaluate
	»
»
PartialDerivative Store


@@ Calculate the gradient of a function.
@@ ( function listOfParametersAndValues -- gradient )
«
	→ f pv
	«
		@@ For each of the parameters ...
		1  pv Size 2 /
		For i
			f pv i  PartialDerivative
		Next

		@@ Combine the partial derivatives.
		pv Size 2 /
		ToArray
	»
»
Gradient Store


@@ Calculate the jacobian of a list of functions.
@@ ( ListOfFunctions listOfParametersAndValues -- Jacobian )
«
	→ f pv
	«
		@@ For each of the functions ...
		1  f Size
		For i
			f  i  Get
			pv  Gradient
		Next

		@@ Combine the gradients.
		f Size
		RowsToMatrix
	»
»
Jacobian Store


@@ Construct a covariance matrix.
@@ ( u R -- CV )
@@
@@ u is a list of uncertainties.
@@ R is a correlation matrix.
«
	→ u R
	«
		@@ Calculate the covariance matrix: CV[i, j] = uu[i, j] * R[i, j].
		@@ Where uu[i, j] = u[i] * u[j].
		@@ This is not a 'dot' operation but element multiplication.
		@@ DB48X does not provide element multiplication for matrices.
		@@ It does for 1 dimensional arrays.

		@@ Transform u into an (n, 1) matrix.
		@@ ( -- u )
		u FromList ToArray 1 ColumnsToMatrix
		@@ Create uu, the outer product of u with u.
		@@ ( -- uu )
		Duplicate Transpose *

		@@ For all i and j: CV[i, j] = uu[i, j] * R[i, j].
		@@ The uu on the stack will be transformed to CV.
		
		@@ ( uu -- uu #rows)
		Duplicate Size
		FromList Drop2

		@@ Process row by row.
		1 Swap
		For i
			@@ ( uu -- uu {i} )
			{} i +
			@@ ( uu {i} -- uu {i} uu[i, *])
			Duplicate2 Get
			@@ ( uu {i} uu[i, *] -- uu {i} uu[i, *] R[i, *] )
			R 3 Pick Get
			@@ CV[i, *] = uu[i, *] * R[i, *].
			@@ ( uu {i} uu[i, *] R[i, *] -- uu/CV )
			* Put
		Next
	»
»
CovarianceMatrixConstruct Store


@@ Calculate the function values and a covariance matrix for:
@@  a list of functions of
@@  a list of parameters and values and
@@  ther correlation matrix of the parameters.
@@
@@ ( ListOfFunctions listOfParameters listOfValues correlationMatrix -- functionValues covarianceMatrix )
@@
@@ The list of functions is a list of (algebraic) expressions.
@@  e.g. { 'a + b + c' 'a * (b + c)' }
@@
@@ The list of parameters is a list of symbols.
@@  e.g. { a b c } 
@@
@@ The list of values is a list of uncertain numbers.
@@  e.g. { 10±σ1 20±σ2 30±σ3 }
@@
@@ The order of the rows and coloms in the correlation matrix
@@  and the order of the lists of parameters and values should match.
@@
@@ The correlation matrix must be completely filled,
@@  not just the upper or lower triangle.
@@
@@ If the functions or/and paramter values have units,
@@  you may get "Inconsistent units" errors.
@@ This comes from taking the derivative
@@  and its poor handling of unit expressions.
@@ The PartialDerivative command catches the error and proceeds
@@  with a algebraic approximation of the derivative.
«
	@@ Extra local variables
	{} {}

	→ f p vu R v u
	«
		@@ Evaluate the functions, ignoring correlation.
		f
		«
			p vu zipper
			Where Evaluate
		»
		Map

		"Uncorrelated values"
		ToTag


		@@ Separate the values with uncertainty in value and uncertainty.
		vu
		«
			If
				Duplicate TypeName "unit" =
			Then
				RangeWithUnitsFrom
			Else
				Object→
			End

			@@ Append (not prepend) to the result lists.
			u Swap + 'u' Store
			v Swap + 'v' Store

			@@ Map needs a result.
			0
		»
		Map
		@@ Don't need the map result, only its side effect (u and v).
		Drop


		@@ Calculate the Jacobian for the functions at the values of the parameters.
		f
		p v zipper
		Jacobian

		@@ Calculate the covariance matrix.
		u R
		CovarianceMatrixConstruct

		@@ Calculate the covariance for the function values: fCV = J * CV * transpose(J).
		Over Transpose * *

		"Covariance (σ²) matrix"
		ToTag
		»
»
PropagateUncertainty Store


@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@
@ Some samples that you can check with mental arithmetic.

{ 'a + b + c' }
{ a b c }
{ 10±σ1 20±σ2 30±σ3 }
[[ 1 0 0 ]
 [ 0 1 0 ]
 [ 0 0 1 ]]

PropagateUncertainty
SquareRoot

@ Expect sqrt(14) = 3.74165 73867 7


@ With correlation between a and b.

{ 'a + b + c' }
{ a b c }
{ 10±σ1 20±σ2 30±σ3 }
[[ 1 1 0 ]
 [ 1 1 0 ]
 [ 0 0 1 ]]

PropagateUncertainty
SquareRoot

@ Expect sqrt(18) = 4.24264 06871 2


@ With negative correlation between b and c.

{ 'a + b + c' }
{ a b c }
{ 10±σ1 20±σ2 30±σ3 }
[[  1  0  0 ]
 [  0  1 -1 ]
 [  0 -1  1 ]]

PropagateUncertainty
SquareRoot

@ Expect sqrt(2) = 1.41421 35623 7


»
